   <!-- Idaho military retirement test case (ID-MIL-02): retired pay with Social Security (#1330
        review, finding 2). Form 39R Part B line 8 reduces the cap by Social Security first and
        only then takes the smaller of the cap and the benefits. -->

   <id>ID-MIL-02</id>
   <tax_year>2025</tax_year>
   <filing_status>S</filing_status>
   <state>ID</state>
   <city>Boise</city>

   <!-- Expected values for validation (2025), worked from the inputs and the 2025 forms (#1330).
        Federal (2025 Form 1040): line 5b military retired pay $30,000 (IRC 61(a)(10)); line 6a
        Social Security $20,000, line 6b taxable part: provisional income 30,000 + 10,000 = 40,000;
        the lesser of 85% x 20,000 = 17,000 and 85% x (40,000 - 34,000) = 5,100 + the lesser of
        10,000 and 4,500 = 9,600 (Pub. 915 worksheet, single). AGI = 30,000 + 9,600 = $39,600.
        Standard deduction $15,750 + $2,000 (born 3/1/1958, 67) = $17,750; senior deduction
        $6,000 (MAGI under $75,000; IRC 63(f)(5)). Taxable income $15,850; tax 1,192.50 + 12% x
        3,925 = $1,663.50.
        Idaho (2025 Form 39R Part B line 8): 8a maximum $48,216 (single); 8b Railroad Retirement
        $0; 8c Social Security $20,000; 8d 48,216 - 20,000 = $28,216; 8e qualifying benefits
        $30,000 (62 or older); 8f the smaller of 8d and 8e = $28,216 -> line 24 -> Form 40 line
        10. The retirement benefits deduction is $28,216, which TestIdahoRetirementBenefitsCapOrder
        pins. Form 39R line 7 also subtracts the $9,600 of Social Security taxed federally, so
        Idaho AGI is 39,600 - 9,600 - 28,216 = $1,784 and Idaho tax $0; that line is not modelled
        and the federal pass puts the whole $20,000 of Social Security into AGI (SPEC 2.9), so
        expected_state_tax is not stated and the AGI check fails until those are fixed. -->
   <expected_agi>39600</expected_agi>
   <expected_taxable_income>15850</expected_taxable_income>
   <expected_total_tax>1663.50</expected_total_tax>

